  "label": "Noise And Bias In Omics",
  "related": [
    "ai-topic-c4ec",
    "annular-mean-passage-f2b7",
    "open-electrophysiological-e8a5"
  ],
  "secondary_categories": [],
  "slug": "metabolic-trait-robustness-microbial-e869",
  "summary": "New preprints model assay and label noise, correct knowledge-graph hub bias, and benchmark tabular and multi-omics survival methods under standardized settings.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is predictive modelling that explicitly accounts for systematic distortions in biological data. DeepCINET models viability-assay noise in cancer cell line pharmacogenomic screens within a ranking framework. siGMA treats label noise and heterogeneity in chemically modified siRNA efficacy through heteroscedastic modelling of aleatoric and epistemic uncertainty. Inverse Node Frequency normalises knowledge-graph metapath features by node degree to reduce hub bias in target-adverse event prediction. SigMatch shows that sample tissue strongly biases transcriptomic disease-relationship inference. A second strand is benchmarking. TabBioMed evaluates 23 models on 94 curated biomedical tabular datasets, where foundation models lead. A comparison of 12 joint dimension reduction methods on 18 TCGA cancers favours JIVE and RGCCA for survival prediction. BI-SCUDO Regression reports noise resistance against LASSO, RF and XGBoost. Drug-response work extends to site-resolved PTM features, spatial transcriptomic signatures and signalling-network synergy simulation (TRAFIKK). The previously cited causal-inference benchmark is no longer represented.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-opus-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-25"
}
